   "id": "g2-12",
   "name": "Man in the tropical zone: exploitation, problems and solutions"
  }
 ],
 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "Methods of exploitation:\n• Nomadic and transhumant herding: the Fulani (Mbororo) move cattle to new pasture in the dry season.\n• Ranching: cattle on fenced pasture (SODEPA ranches in Adamawa).\n• Crop farming in the rainy season: millet, sorghum, groundnuts, maize; cash crops like cotton (SODECOTON).\n• Irrigated farming in the dry season: rice at Yagoua and Maga (SEMRY), onions.\n• Hunting, fishing (Lake Chad, Lagdo), tourism (Waza, Bénoué).\nProblems: drought and irregular rain, desertification, overgrazing, bush fires, soil erosion, conflicts between farmers and herders, tsetse flies, poor roads.\nSolutions: tree planting (the Green Sahel operation), irrigation and dams, controlled grazing, fighting bush fires, improved seeds, vaccinating animals, better roads.",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {
   "p": "overgrazing"
  },
  "problem": "How does {p} damage the savanna, and what is one solution?",
  "steps": [
   "Too many animals eat the grass faster than it can grow.",
   "The bare soil is eroded by wind and rain, leading to desertification."
  ],
  "answer": "It leaves the soil bare and eroded; a solution is controlled grazing (fewer animals, rotating pasture).",
  "checks": []
 },
